@code {

    public static readonly RenderFragment Introduction = __builder =>
    {
        <Paragraph>
            In this chapter we bridge the Rust side of the dialect with the MLIR infrastructure and transfer
            the whole logic from MLIR Toy Tutorial Chapter 2 into Rust code.
        </Paragraph>
    };

}

<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.MakingLanguagesInRust.Posts[3]">
    @Introduction

    <Paragraph>
        Since MLIR is fully
        extensible, all dialects that are used need to be loaded into an MLIR Context to actually enable verification,
        optimisation passes, etc. In the C++ API this is done via registering all operations into a class inheriting
        from <Code>Dialect</Code> (<Code>ToyDialect</Code> in our case) and then loading it into the context with:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" Code="context.loadDialect<toy::ToyDialect>()">
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Now the problem here is that Melior's <Code>dialect</Code> macro
        did not generate a <Code>ToyDialect</Code> struct for us. Indeed, how on
        earth are we supposed to actually load our dialect with Melior? Unfortunately, we need to look at how MLIR
        normally works with C++ and then hack our way around Rust/C++ interop.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header2>Let the Hacks Begin</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        We need to register the dialect, which means we need to generate the <Code>ToyDialect</Code> class in C++,
        create a function that registers it to an MLIR Context, and call it from Rust. If you're not familiar with
        Rust/C++ interop, well you're about to be. I'm not going to be using any fancy tools for bindings &ndash;
        ideally, all of the hacky glue code we're about to see would be autogenerated anyway. We'll use standard
        C FFI.
        <Footnote>Foreign Function Interface. Turns out, calling functions is actually hard work and different
            languages do it differently. In particular, Rust doesn't even have a stable FFI, which means the way
            functions are exposed and arguments are passed can change between Rust versions. When communicating between
            languages people tend to use the C FFI, because it's relatively simple and well-defined. In both C++ and
            Rust we define those functions in an <Code>extern "C"</Code> block; they are limited to simple numeric
            types, pointers, and structs with a fixed C layout.
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        By the guiding principle of minimising pain, let's get a minimal solution that will allow us to register
        the dialect before we do the codegen. Recall that in the <Code>MlirGenerator</Code> we explicitly told MLIR
        to allow unrecognised dialects with <Code>set_allow_unregistered_dialects(true)</Code>. Let's first change
        that part of the code:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/codegen.rs" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="94"
               Code=@(@"
// Register all standard dialects.
let registry = DialectRegistry::new();
register_all_dialects(&registry);

context.append_dialect_registry(&registry);
context.load_all_available_dialects();

// Load our dialect and perform all the sorcery required to connect Rust and C++ code.
toy::load_dialect(&context);
// Make sure the context recognizes operations from our dialect.
assert!(context.is_registered_operation(""toy.constant""));
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        This will fail now, since we need to implement <Code>toy::load_dialect</Code> first. To that end, we need
        to generate the C++ side of things and call into it from Rust. More precisely, we need a C++ project
        with a CMakeLists that will perform the usual MLIR
        TableGen into C++ classes and emit a library that we can link from Rust. Here's the CMake configuration:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cmake" FileName="src/dialect/CMakeLists.txt" LineNumbers="true" Code=@(@"
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20.0)
project(toy-dialect)

set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23)

find_package(LLVM REQUIRED)
set(MLIR_DIR ""${LLVM_DIR}/../mlir"")
find_package(MLIR REQUIRED)

set(LLVM_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_INTDIR ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/bin)
set(LLVM_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_INTDIR ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/lib)
set(MLIR_BINARY_DIR ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR})
list(APPEND CMAKE_MODULE_PATH ""${MLIR_CMAKE_DIR}"")
list(APPEND CMAKE_MODULE_PATH ""${LLVM_CMAKE_DIR}"")
include(TableGen)
include(AddLLVM)
include(AddMLIR)
include_directories(${LLVM_INCLUDE_DIRS})
include_directories(${MLIR_INCLUDE_DIRS})
link_directories(${LLVM_BUILD_LIBRARY_DIR})
add_definitions(${LLVM_DEFINITIONS})

add_mlir_dialect(ToyOps toy)
add_mlir_doc(ToyOps ToyDialect Dialects/ -gen-dialect-doc)

set(LLVM_TARGET_DEFINITIONS toy.td)
mlir_tablegen(Ops.h.inc -gen-op-decls)
mlir_tablegen(Ops.cpp.inc -gen-op-defs)
mlir_tablegen(Dialect.h.inc -gen-dialect-decls)
mlir_tablegen(Dialect.cpp.inc -gen-dialect-defs)
add_public_tablegen_target(ToyIncGen)

include_directories(${PROJECT_BINARY_DIR})

add_dependencies(mlir-headers ToyIncGen)
add_library(Toy toy.cpp)
llvm_update_compile_flags(Toy)
add_dependencies(Toy ToyIncGen)
target_link_libraries(Toy
  PRIVATE
    MLIRAnalysis
    MLIRFunctionInterfaces
    MLIRIR
    MLIRParser
    MLIRSideEffectInterfaces
    MLIRTransforms)
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Most of these are magic spells required to make LLVM, MLIR, and TableGen work at all. They're not really
        documented anywhere as far as I can te
        <Footnote>Because why would they be, you're a C++ user, you like pain, right?</Footnote>
        ,
        but in short we add all of the required libraries, point towards our <Code>toy.td</Code> file, ask MLIR to
        to generate a bunch of files (<Code>Ops</Code> and <Code>Dialect</Code>, both header and <Code>cpp</Code> file),
        create a <Code>Toy</Code> library target from our <Code>toy.cpp</Code> file (see below), and finally link
        all that together with MLIR. This should produce a <Code>libToy.a</Code> shared library we'll be able
        to link from Rust.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        To have that done for us automatically and not have to use two separate build systems, we use the
        <LinkA Href="https://github.com/rust-lang/cmake-rs">cmake</LinkA>
        crate to trigger the C++ build from
        <Code>build.rs</Code> and have all files put in the usual cargo output. We expand the <Code>build.rs</Code>
        to generate both the lalrpop parser, and build and link the C++ library.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LineNumbers="true" FileName="build.rs" Code=@(@"
//! Setup the code generation for the crate:
//!   - MLIR dialect generation using cmake;
//!   - lalrpop parser generation from `.lalrpop`.
fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    setup_mlir_dialect();
    setup_lalrpop()?;
    Ok(())
}

fn setup_lalrpop() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    println!(""cargo::rerun-if-changed=src/parser/syntax.lalrpop"");
    lalrpop::process_src()
}

fn setup_mlir_dialect() {
    let dst = cmake::Config::new(""src/dialect"").build_target(""Toy"").build();
    println!(""cargo:rustc-link-search=native={}/build"", dst.display());
    println!(""cargo:rustc-link-lib=static=Toy"");

    println!(""cargo::rerun-if-changed=src/dialect.rs"");
    println!(""cargo::rerun-if-changed=src/dialect"");
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        The <Code>cmake</Code> crate does the C++ build, while the cargo directive
        <Code>rustc-link-lib=static=Toy</Code> actually tells rustc to link <Code>libToy.a</Code> to our Rust crate.
        This will allow us to call extern symbols from that library in Rust.
        <Footnote>If you've never done C++ interop
            before, this is a pretty straightforward method that you can apply &ndash; create a static lib with extern
            functions and then equivalent extern declarations on Rust side. For more complicated projects
            you probably want to use
            <LinkA Href="https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-bindgen">bindgen</LinkA>
            .
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        In the <Code>toy.cpp</Code> file I am &ndash; for the time being &ndash; putting completely empty definitions of
        required
        <Code>verify</Code>, <Code>print</Code>, and <Code>parse</Code> functions for all ops. We will fix that later.
        First things first, we expose a
        <Code>toyLoadDialect</Code> function that takes an MLIR Context and registers the dialect for us:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" FileName="src/dialect/toy.cpp" Code=@(@"
void mlir::toy::ToyDialect::initialize() {
  addOperations<
#define GET_OP_LIST
#include ""Ops.cpp.inc""

      >();
}

// Exported to Rust.
extern ""C"" {
mlir::toy::ToyDialect *toyLoadDialect(mlir::MLIRContext *context) {
  auto *dialect = context->getOrLoadDialect<mlir::toy::ToyDialect>();
  return dialect;
}")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        The <Code>initialize</Code> function is some eldritch C++ magic,
        <Footnote>The <Code>Ops.cpp.inc</Code> file
            contains a magical <Code>ifdef</Code> that checks for the <Code>GET_OP_LIST</Code> directive
            and then causes the file to just contain a comma-separated list of operation class names. Since <Code>#include</Code>
            in C++ just textually pastes the contents of the file wherever it occurs, this automatically resolves to
            an <Code>addOperations</Code> call with the template parameters being all the operation class names.
            Absolutely nasty stuff, but it is the &ldquo;idiomatic&rdquo; way to do this with MLIR&hellip;
        </Footnote>
        but it simply registers all Toy ops to the Toy dialect. In <Code>toy.rs</Code> we now put the Rust side of the
        equation:
        <Footnote>Note the SAFETY comment above the <Code>unsafe</Code> block. Putting those is enforced
            by an optional Clippy lint, <Code>undocumented_unsafe_blocks</Code>, that I've set to warn. This makes
            sure that you are aware of all the invariants needed for soundness when using unsafe operations &ndash;
            since you need to justify it in the comment &ndash; and also indirectly
            discourages using <Code>unsafe</Code> blocks &ndash; cause you have to type those comments and that's
            annoying.
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/dialect/toy.rs" Code=@(@"
/// Glue code for registering the dialect and verification functions with C++.
mod ffi {
    use std::ffi::c_void;

    unsafe extern ""C"" {
        pub(super) fn toyLoadDialect(context: *mut c_void) -> *mut c_void;
    }
}

/// Load the Toy dialect to enable use within the given context.
///
/// This is a one-shot full initialization, the dialect and all its operations are usable
/// after this function returns.
pub(crate) fn load_dialect(context: &melior::Context) -> melior::dialect::Dialect {
    // SAFETY: the ptr inside melior::Context is guaranteed to be valid
    unsafe {
        let ptr = ffi::toyLoadDialect(context.to_raw().ptr);
        melior::dialect::Dialect::from_raw(mlir_sys::MlirDialect { ptr })
    }
}")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        We can't really strongly type the <Code>extern</Code> pointers, so this will have to do. We can run our code now
        and it will work, but it will print an empty module since all of the <Code>print</Code> impls in
        <Code>toy.cpp</Code> are empty. However, that means we <em>did</em> successfully load the dialect, because
        the assertion we put in the codegen module passed. Now to get pretty printing we could just implement the
        <Code>print</Code> functions in C++, but that would be against the spirit of our little adventure here. Instead,
        we go the hard and rusty way.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header2>You Gotta Rust It Up</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        We want to call C++ code from Rust &ndash; we need to load the dialect and when doing verification of printing
        we eventually need to call the C++ functions defined for ops &ndash; but we also need C++ to call into Rust
        &ndash; since we want the implementations of <Code>verify</Code>/<Code>print</Code>/<Code>parse</Code> to be
        in Rust. This is a circular dependency and that's Bad Times&trade; &ndash; we can't have two statically linked
        libraries that call into each other. We're going to solve this by manually implementing virtual dispatch
        with virtual function tables.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        If you're not aware of how virtual functions are implemented, here's a quick rundown. An object participating
        in an inheritance hierarchy that has virtual functions has an additional pointer as part of its in-memory
        structure that points to a
        <Highlight>vtable</Highlight>
        . The vtable has pointers to functions for each virtual
        function of the object. That way, when we have a pointer of a superclass type on which a virtual function is
        called, we can load the vtable, the relevant function pointer from it, and call the concrete implementation
        of the function.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        What I want here is to have a table of function implementations for each op type in our dialect.
        That way, when the C++ function on the operation is called, we can lookup the function pointer and call to the
        actual implementation, written in Rust. I'm going to call this structure <Highlight>rtable</Highlight>,
        for Rust table, and in C++ it'll look like this:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" FileName="dialect/rust_dialect.hpp" Code=@(@"
// Definition of helper types - the Rust function table and signatures.
//
// Interop is achieved by Rust calling into C++ and passing function pointers to
// all relevant pieces of logic for all operations in the dialect. C++ puts this
// information into a RustOpTable and then calls back to Rust via these
// pointers.
#pragma once
#include <vector>

namespace mlir {
using PrintOp = void(mlir::OpAsmPrinter *printer, mlir::Operation *op);

// Table of registered Rust functions.
struct RustOpTable {
  // Rust code for Operation::print.
  PrintOp *print;
};

class RustDialectShim {
public:
  void initialize(size_t num_ops) { rtables.resize(num_ops); }

  void registerRustOp(size_t op, RustOpTable table) { rtables[op] = table; }

  void printOp(size_t opName, mlir::Operation *op,
               mlir::OpAsmPrinter *printer) const {
    auto mbPrint = rtables[opName].print;
    if (mbPrint != nullptr) {
      mbPrint(printer, op);
    } else {
      printer->printGenericOp(op);
    }
  }

private:
  std::vector<RustOpTable> rtables{};
};
} // namespace mlir")>
    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We hold the rtables for all ops, assuming there is a mapping from the op types to integers from $0$ to
        $\mathit{num_ops} - 1$. To get that mapping we reuse the MLIR header that gives us a list of op names to
        generate an enum:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" FileName="dialect/toy.hpp">

    </CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Now to hook this into the actual dialect class we'll use TableGen to add code to the generated
        <Code>ToyDialect</Code> class:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="python" LanguageString="TableGen" FileName="dialect/toy.td" LineNumbers="true"
               LineNumbersStartAt="21" Code=@(@"
// Provide a definition of the 'toy' dialect in the ODS framework so that we
// can define our operations.
def Toy_Dialect : Dialect {
  let name = ""toy"";
  let cppNamespace = ""::mlir::toy"";

  let extraClassDeclaration = [{
    public:
        RustDialectShim& rustDialect() { return rustDialectShim; }
    private:
        RustDialectShim rustDialectShim{};
  }];
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We need to initialise the shim in the <Code>toyLoadDialect</Code> function, and, unfortunately, add boilerplate
        to register rtables for all operations from Rust. The rtable is a simple C struct, so we can pass it all
        directly through ffi.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" FileName="dialect/toy.cpp" Code=@(@"
// Exported to Rust.
extern ""C"" {
mlir::toy::ToyDialect *toyLoadDialect(mlir::MLIRContext *context) {
  auto *dialect = context->getOrLoadDialect<mlir::toy::ToyDialect>();
  dialect->rustDialect().initialize(mlir::toy::ToyNumOps);
  return dialect;
}

void registerAddOpRustTable(mlir::toy::ToyDialect *dialect,
                            mlir::RustOpTable rtable) {
  dialect->rustDialect().registerRustOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Add, rtable);
}
void registerConstantOpRustTable(mlir::toy::ToyDialect *dialect,
                            mlir::RustOpTable rtable) {
  dialect->rustDialect().registerRustOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Constant, rtable);
}
/* ... ✂️ snip ... */
void registerTransposeOpRustTable(mlir::toy::ToyDialect *dialect,
                            mlir::RustOpTable rtable) {
  dialect->rustDialect().registerRustOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Transpose, rtable);
}
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We can now replace the empty <Code>print</Code> implementations with calls to <Code>printOp</Code> via the shim:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="cpp" FileName="dialect/toy.cpp" Code=@(@"

void mlir::toy::AddOp::print(mlir::OpAsmPrinter &printer) {
  auto dialect = static_cast<mlir::toy::ToyDialect *>(
      getContext()->getLoadedDialect(""toy""));
  dialect->rustDialect().printOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Add, getOperation(),
                                 &printer);
}
void mlir::toy::ConstantOp::print(mlir::OpAsmPrinter &printer) {
  auto dialect = static_cast<mlir::toy::ToyDialect *>(
      getContext()->getLoadedDialect(""toy""));
  dialect->rustDialect().printOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Constant, getOperation(),
                                 &printer);
/* ... ✂️ snip ... */
void mlir::toy::TransposeOp::print(mlir::OpAsmPrinter &printer) {
  auto dialect = static_cast<mlir::toy::ToyDialect *>(
      getContext()->getLoadedDialect(""toy""));
  dialect->rustDialect().printOp(mlir::toy::ToyOp::Transpose, getOperation(),
                                 &printer);
}")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        To hook it up from Rust we need to define the FFI types and then define the extern functions' signatures.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/dialect/ffi.rs" Code=@(@"
//! FFI utilities for C++ interop regarding MLIR dialects.
use std::ffi::c_void;

#[repr(C)]
struct RustOpTable {
    /// Rust code for Operation::print.
    print: PrintOp
}

/// Type for `print` operations. Functions that take the operation to print and the printer.
pub(super) type PrintOp = unsafe extern ""C"" fn(*mut c_void, *mut c_void);
")></CodeBlock>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/dialect/ffi.rs" Code=@(@"
/// Glue code for registering the dialect and verification functions with C++.
mod ffi {
    use super::super::ffi::RustOpTable;
    use std::ffi::c_void;

    unsafe extern ""C"" {
        pub(super) fn toyLoadDialect(context: *mut c_void) -> *mut c_void;
        pub(super) fn registerAddOpRustTable(dialect: *mut c_void, rtable: RustOpTable);
        pub(super) fn registerConstantOpRustTable(dialect: *mut c_void, rtable: RustOpTable);
        /* ... ✂️ snip ... */
        pub(super) fn registerTransposeOpRustTable(dialect: *mut c_void, rtable: RustOpTable);
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Now we need to actually define the printing logic.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header2>Printing</Header2>
    <Paragraph>
        In the C++ API we have the <Code>OpAsmPrinter</Code> which gives us a bunch of quality-of-life functions
        to make printing easier. For example, <Code>printFunctionalType</Code> prints a type in the nice
        <Code>(op1, op2..., opn) -> (r1, r2, ..., rm)</Code> format. We'll need to reimplement those in Rust
        and then the plan is to construct the string for an operation on Rust side and tell the C++ printer to print
        the entire string.
    </Paragraph>
</BlogPost>
